@namespace Lumeo
@using System.ComponentModel
@using System.Globalization
@using Lumeo.GanttV3
@inject Lumeo.Services.Localization.ILumeoLocalizer L
@attribute [EditorBrowsable(EditorBrowsableState.Never)]

@* GanttSettingsMenu — a companion popover (design spec Phase 3, T8 — "Look &
   configurability audit": every REUI settings toggle exposed as a component
   parameter, PLUS this menu so consumers get the REUI-demo experience out of
   the box) exposing Display/Behavior/Region/Style toggles for a GanttChart
   instance. Slots into GanttChart.TrailingContent (-> GanttNav.TrailingContent),
   e.g.:
     <GanttChart ShowSummaryBars="@_showSummaryBars" ...>
         <TrailingContent>
             <GanttSettingsMenu @bind-ShowSummaryBars="_showSummaryBars" ... />
         </TrailingContent>
     </GanttChart>

   DECISION 1 (state-first vs. explicit two-way parameters — design spec
   Phase 3, T8 dispatch: "decide, record"): explicit two-way parameters WIN,
   UNCONDITIONALLY — this component accepts NO GanttState parameter at all.
   Researched first (T7 decision 5's own precedent: research can replace a
   plan's literal wording when the wording doesn't hold up against the actual
   code): GanttState (see its own class remarks) models exactly Tasks/
   ViewMode/VisibleRange/Collapsed/SelectedIds — the REUI useGanttState
   "shared nav/view state" surface — and NONE of the eleven Display/Behavior/
   Region/Style settings this menu manages. Every one of them is ALREADY a
   plain GanttChart [Parameter] with GanttChart's OWN parameters as their one true
   owner. Retrofitting GanttState with eleven more properties (and GanttChart
   with the controlled/uncontrolled plumbing to read them back) would be
   EXACTLY the "third, inconsistent way to own the same values" the T8
   dispatch itself warns against — GanttChart's plain parameters would become a
   SECOND owner alongside a new GanttState-hosted one, not a replacement —
   and it would touch ~2500 lines of reconcile-critical GanttChart code that 18+
   Codex review rounds hardened, for a companion-COMPONENT task. So "state-
   first, params override" resolves the only coherent way available: there
   is no state to defer to, so this component's own two-way parameters are
   authoritative. A consumer wires the SAME backing field to both GanttChart
   (one-way) and this menu (two-way) — precisely the "lifted state" pattern
   every other GanttChart controlled/uncontrolled pair already asks of a
   consumer building custom surrounding UI (TreePaneWidth/SelectedIds).

   Controlled-ness idiom, chosen per parameter TYPE, not applied uniformly
   (a second, equally deliberate decision worth recording alongside #1):
   - The nine plain `bool` settings (no natural "unset" sentinel — `false`
     is a fully meaningful explicit value) gate controlled-ness on their OWN
     Changed callback's HasDelegate, mirroring Lumeo's OWN Popover.Open/
     Checkbox.Checked idiom for exactly this situation.
   - FirstDayOfWeek (DayOfWeek?) and OffDays (IReadOnlySet<DayOfWeek>?) gate
     on the PARAMETER's own nullability instead, mirroring GanttChart.TreePaneWidth
     — both types have a genuine, meaningful "unset" value (null = culture-
     derived) to use as the sentinel, the same reason TreePaneWidth's own
     T5 disable-check fix moved OFF HasDelegate-gating (a caller wiring only
     Changed to OBSERVE, while leaving the value itself null/uncontrolled,
     must not get silently frozen). Using nullability where it is
     semantically available is the STRICTER, bug-resistant option; using
     HasDelegate where it is NOT available (a plain bool) is the only viable
     option, not a lesser one.

   DECISION 2 ("Reset to defaults" semantics): restores each setting to the
   value THIS component instance had at its OWN first render (captured once,
   OnInitialized — before any user interaction), NOT this library's hardcoded
   parameter defaults. For an uncontrolled field the two coincide (nothing
   else could have set it before mount). They diverge, in the RIGHT
   direction, for a CONTROLLED field: a consumer's app very often has its own
   sensible starting configuration (e.g. ShowSummaryBars="true" by default in
   THEIR product) — "reset" restoring the library's bare `false` instead
   would silently fight the consumer's own app default, not the user's own
   session changes, which is what a settings-panel reset button
   conventionally means (the same convention a per-document "restore
   defaults" panel uses: back to how THIS view/document started, not a
   whole-product factory reset). It is also the only option that does not
   need this component to duplicate GanttChart's own default constants a second,
   driftable time. *@
<Popover>
    <PopoverTrigger AsChild>
        <Button Variant="Button.ButtonVariant.Outline" Size="Button.ButtonSize.Icon" aria-label="@L["Gantt.Settings"]" title="@L["Gantt.Settings"]">
            <SvgGlyph Svg="@(LumeoIcons.Settings2)" class="h-4 w-4" />
        </Button>
    </PopoverTrigger>
    <PopoverContent Align="Lumeo.Align.End" Class="w-80 max-h-[70vh] overflow-y-auto" @attributes="AdditionalAttributes">
        @* @key="_commitGeneration" (see the field's own remarks): forces Blazor
           to treat this whole body as a NEW logical instance after every
           committed toggle, sidestepping a real Blazor/Checkbox rendering
           quirk — see _commitGeneration's own remarks for the full mechanism
           and why this is necessary for the controlled-veto contract. *@
        <div class="@RootClass" @key="_commitGeneration">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2">
                <span class="text-sm font-semibold text-foreground">@L["Gantt.Settings"]</span>
                <Button Variant="Button.ButtonVariant.Ghost" Size="Button.ButtonSize.Sm" OnClick="ResetAsync" data-testid="gantt-settings-reset">
                    <SvgGlyph Svg="@(LumeoIcons.RotateCcw)" class="h-3.5 w-3.5 me-1.5" />
                    @L["Gantt.SettingsReset"]
                </Button>
            </div>

            <div role="group" aria-labelledby="@_displayHeadingId" class="flex flex-col gap-2.5">
                <div id="@_displayHeadingId" class="text-xs font-semibold text-muted-foreground uppercase tracking-wide">@L["Gantt.SettingsDisplay"]</div>
                <Checkbox Label="@L["Gantt.SettingsShowRowCheckboxes"]" Checked="@EffectiveShowRowCheckboxes" CheckedChanged="@(v => SetShowRowCheckboxesAsync(v))" />
                <Checkbox Label="@L["Gantt.SettingsShowSummaryBars"]" Checked="@EffectiveShowSummaryBars" CheckedChanged="@(v => SetShowSummaryBarsAsync(v))" />
                <Checkbox Label="@L["Gantt.SettingsShowZoomControl"]" Checked="@EffectiveShowZoomControl" CheckedChanged="@(v => SetShowZoomControlAsync(v))" />
                <Checkbox Label="@L["Gantt.SettingsShowOffscreenIndicators"]" Checked="@EffectiveShowOffscreenIndicators" CheckedChanged="@(v => SetShowOffscreenIndicatorsAsync(v))" />
                @* T9 wiring point filled (design spec Phase 3, T9): matches
                   the plan's own Display-group ordering "checkboxes/summary/
                   zoom/off-screen chips/infinite scroll/now/off-days" —
                   immediately below ShowOffscreenIndicators, above
                   NowIndicator. Default true — mirrors GanttChart.InfiniteScroll's
                   OWN default-true (same "this menu's uncontrolled fallback
                   matches GanttChart's real default deliberately" precedent
                   ShowOffscreenIndicators above already established), so a
                   menu rendered before any user interaction reflects reality
                   even when unbound. *@
                <Checkbox Label="@L["Gantt.SettingsInfiniteScroll"]" Checked="@EffectiveInfiniteScroll" CheckedChanged="@(v => SetInfiniteScrollAsync(v))" />
                <Checkbox Label="@L["Gantt.SettingsNowIndicator"]" Checked="@EffectiveNowIndicator" CheckedChanged="@(v => SetNowIndicatorAsync(v))" />
                <Checkbox Label="@L["Gantt.SettingsMarkOffDays"]" Checked="@EffectiveMarkOffDays" CheckedChanged="@(v => SetMarkOffDaysAsync(v))" />
            </div>

            <Separator />

            <div role="group" aria-labelledby="@_behaviorHeadingId" class="flex flex-col gap-2.5">
                <div id="@_behaviorHeadingId" class="text-xs font-semibold text-muted-foreground uppercase tracking-wide">@L["Gantt.SettingsBehavior"]</div>
                <Checkbox Label="@L["Gantt.SettingsReadonly"]" Checked="@EffectiveReadonly" CheckedChanged="@(v => SetReadonlyAsync(v))" />
                <Checkbox Label="@L["Gantt.SettingsAllowCreate"]" Checked="@EffectiveAllowCreate" CheckedChanged="@(v => SetAllowCreateAsync(v))" />
                <Checkbox Label="@L["Gantt.SettingsAllowRowReorder"]" Checked="@EffectiveAllowRowReorder" CheckedChanged="@(v => SetAllowRowReorderAsync(v))" />
            </div>

            <Separator />

            <div role="group" aria-labelledby="@_regionHeadingId" class="flex flex-col gap-2.5">
                <div id="@_regionHeadingId" class="text-xs font-semibold text-muted-foreground uppercase tracking-wide">@L["Gantt.SettingsRegion"]</div>
                <div class="flex flex-col gap-1.5">
                    <span class="text-sm text-foreground">@L["Gantt.SettingsFirstDayOfWeek"]</span>
                    <ToggleGroup Type="ToggleGroup.ToggleGroupType.Single" Variant="ToggleGroup.ToggleGroupVariant.Outline" Size="Lumeo.Size.Sm"
                                 Value="@DisplayFirstDayOfWeek.ToString()" ValueChanged="HandleFirstDayOfWeekToggledAsync">
                        @foreach (var day in WeekDays)
                        {
                            <ToggleGroupItem Value="@day.ToString()" AriaLabel="@FullDayName(day)">@AbbreviatedDayName(day)</ToggleGroupItem>
                        }
                    </ToggleGroup>
                </div>
                <div class="flex flex-col gap-1.5">
                    <span class="text-sm text-foreground">@L["Gantt.SettingsOffDays"]</span>
                    <ToggleGroup Type="ToggleGroup.ToggleGroupType.Multiple" Variant="ToggleGroup.ToggleGroupVariant.Outline" Size="Lumeo.Size.Sm"
                                 SelectedValues="@DisplayOffDays.Select(d => d.ToString())" SelectedValuesChanged="HandleOffDaysToggledAsync">
                        @foreach (var day in WeekDays)
                        {
                            <ToggleGroupItem Value="@day.ToString()" AriaLabel="@FullDayName(day)">@AbbreviatedDayName(day)</ToggleGroupItem>
                        }
                    </ToggleGroup>
                </div>
            </div>

            <Separator />

            <div role="group" aria-labelledby="@_styleHeadingId" class="flex flex-col gap-2.5">
                <div id="@_styleHeadingId" class="text-xs font-semibold text-muted-foreground uppercase tracking-wide">@L["Gantt.SettingsStyle"]</div>
                <Checkbox Label="@L["Gantt.SettingsColorByGroup"]" Checked="@EffectiveColorByGroup" CheckedChanged="@(v => SetColorByGroupAsync(v))" />
            </div>
        </div>
    </PopoverContent>
</Popover>

@code {
    // ── Display group ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>Two-way bindable — mirrors <see cref="GanttChart.ShowRowCheckboxes"/>. Uncontrolled default <c>false</c>.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool ShowRowCheckboxes { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<bool> ShowRowCheckboxesChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Two-way bindable — mirrors <see cref="GanttChart.ShowSummaryBars"/>. Uncontrolled default <c>false</c>.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool ShowSummaryBars { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<bool> ShowSummaryBarsChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Two-way bindable — mirrors <see cref="GanttChart.ShowZoomControl"/>. Uncontrolled default <c>false</c>.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool ShowZoomControl { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<bool> ShowZoomControlChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Two-way bindable — mirrors <see cref="GanttChart.ShowOffscreenIndicators"/>,
    /// INCLUDING its default-<c>true</c> (this menu's own uncontrolled fallback
    /// matches GanttChart's real default deliberately, so a menu rendered before any
    /// user interaction reflects reality even when unbound).
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool ShowOffscreenIndicators { get; set; } = true;
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<bool> ShowOffscreenIndicatorsChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Two-way bindable — mirrors <see cref="GanttChart.InfiniteScroll"/>, INCLUDING
    /// its default-<c>true</c> (design spec Phase 3, T9 — same "this menu's
    /// uncontrolled fallback matches GanttChart's real default deliberately"
    /// precedent <see cref="ShowOffscreenIndicators"/> above already
    /// established), so a menu rendered before any user interaction reflects
    /// reality even when unbound.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool InfiniteScroll { get; set; } = true;
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<bool> InfiniteScrollChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Two-way bindable — mirrors <see cref="GanttChart.NowIndicator"/>. Uncontrolled default <c>false</c>.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool NowIndicator { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<bool> NowIndicatorChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Two-way bindable — mirrors <see cref="GanttChart.MarkOffDays"/>. Uncontrolled default <c>false</c>.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool MarkOffDays { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<bool> MarkOffDaysChanged { get; set; }

    // ── Behavior group ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>Two-way bindable — mirrors <see cref="GanttChart.Readonly"/>. Uncontrolled default <c>false</c>.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Readonly { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<bool> ReadonlyChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Two-way bindable — mirrors <see cref="GanttChart.AllowCreate"/>. Uncontrolled default <c>false</c>.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool AllowCreate { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<bool> AllowCreateChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Two-way bindable — mirrors <see cref="GanttChart.AllowRowReorder"/>. Uncontrolled default <c>false</c>.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool AllowRowReorder { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<bool> AllowRowReorderChanged { get; set; }

    // ── Region group ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// Two-way bindable — mirrors <see cref="GanttChart.FirstDayOfWeek"/>. Controlled-ness
    /// is decided by THIS parameter's own nullability (not
    /// <see cref="FirstDayOfWeekChanged"/>'s HasDelegate — see the class remarks for
    /// why nullable settings use this idiom, unlike the plain-bool ones above). Null
    /// (the default, both as a parameter and as this menu's own internal fallback) is
    /// "culture-derived" — the toggle group still shows the resolved day pressed (see
    /// <see cref="DisplayFirstDayOfWeek"/>), it is simply not an EXPLICIT choice yet.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public DayOfWeek? FirstDayOfWeek { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<DayOfWeek?> FirstDayOfWeekChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Two-way bindable — mirrors <see cref="GanttChart.OffDays"/>. Same nullability-based
    /// controlled-ness as <see cref="FirstDayOfWeek"/>. Null is "culture-derived" (see
    /// <see cref="DisplayOffDays"/> for what actually renders pressed); an EXPLICIT
    /// empty set (every day toggled off) is a genuinely different, valid value — "zero
    /// off days" — and is never silently coerced back to null (see
    /// <see cref="HandleOffDaysToggledAsync"/>'s own remarks).
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public IReadOnlySet<DayOfWeek>? OffDays { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<IReadOnlySet<DayOfWeek>?> OffDaysChanged { get; set; }

    // ── Style group ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>Two-way bindable — mirrors <see cref="GanttChart.ColorByGroup"/>. Uncontrolled default <c>false</c>.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool ColorByGroup { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<bool> ColorByGroupChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Additional CSS classes merged onto the popover panel's inner content wrapper.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Captures unmatched attributes and applies them to the popover panel.</summary>
    [Parameter(CaptureUnmatchedValues = true)] public Dictionary<string, object>? AdditionalAttributes { get; set; }

    private string RootClass => Cx.Merge("flex flex-col gap-3", Class);

    // Lumeo.Internal.LumeoIds (the core project's own id-generator) is
    // internal to the core Lumeo assembly and not visible from this one
    // (Lumeo.Gantt) — no InternalsVisibleTo grants it (see GanttBar.razor's
    // own identical remark on its own _barId) — so this inlines the same
    // "{prefix}-{guidN}" format directly instead.
    private readonly string _displayHeadingId = $"gantt-settings-display-{Guid.NewGuid():N}";
    private readonly string _behaviorHeadingId = $"gantt-settings-behavior-{Guid.NewGuid():N}";
    private readonly string _regionHeadingId = $"gantt-settings-region-{Guid.NewGuid():N}";
    private readonly string _styleHeadingId = $"gantt-settings-style-{Guid.NewGuid():N}";

    // Uncontrolled backing store — only ever read/written when the matching
    // Changed callback has no delegate (see the class remarks' controlled-ness
    // idiom split). Seeded to the SAME defaults the parameters themselves
    // declare above, so an entirely-unbound menu (no parameters at all —
    // unlikely in practice, but the simplest self-consistency check) renders
    // identically to one whose parameters were explicitly passed their own
    // declared defaults.
    private bool _internalShowRowCheckboxes;
    private bool _internalShowSummaryBars;
    private bool _internalShowZoomControl;
    private bool _internalShowOffscreenIndicators = true;
    private bool _internalInfiniteScroll = true;
    private bool _internalNowIndicator;
    private bool _internalMarkOffDays;
    private bool _internalReadonly;
    private bool _internalAllowCreate;
    private bool _internalAllowRowReorder;
    private bool _internalColorByGroup;
    private DayOfWeek? _internalFirstDayOfWeek;
    private IReadOnlySet<DayOfWeek>? _internalOffDays;

    private bool EffectiveShowRowCheckboxes => ShowRowCheckboxesChanged.HasDelegate ? ShowRowCheckboxes : _internalShowRowCheckboxes;
    private bool EffectiveShowSummaryBars => ShowSummaryBarsChanged.HasDelegate ? ShowSummaryBars : _internalShowSummaryBars;
    private bool EffectiveShowZoomControl => ShowZoomControlChanged.HasDelegate ? ShowZoomControl : _internalShowZoomControl;
    private bool EffectiveShowOffscreenIndicators => ShowOffscreenIndicatorsChanged.HasDelegate ? ShowOffscreenIndicators : _internalShowOffscreenIndicators;
    private bool EffectiveInfiniteScroll => InfiniteScrollChanged.HasDelegate ? InfiniteScroll : _internalInfiniteScroll;
    private bool EffectiveNowIndicator => NowIndicatorChanged.HasDelegate ? NowIndicator : _internalNowIndicator;
    private bool EffectiveMarkOffDays => MarkOffDaysChanged.HasDelegate ? MarkOffDays : _internalMarkOffDays;
    private bool EffectiveReadonly => ReadonlyChanged.HasDelegate ? Readonly : _internalReadonly;
    private bool EffectiveAllowCreate => AllowCreateChanged.HasDelegate ? AllowCreate : _internalAllowCreate;
    private bool EffectiveAllowRowReorder => AllowRowReorderChanged.HasDelegate ? AllowRowReorder : _internalAllowRowReorder;
    private bool EffectiveColorByGroup => ColorByGroupChanged.HasDelegate ? ColorByGroup : _internalColorByGroup;
    private DayOfWeek? EffectiveFirstDayOfWeek => FirstDayOfWeek ?? _internalFirstDayOfWeek;
    private IReadOnlySet<DayOfWeek>? EffectiveOffDays => OffDays ?? _internalOffDays;

    // Display-only resolution of the two Region settings — what the toggle
    // groups actually show pressed. EffectiveFirstDayOfWeek/EffectiveOffDays
    // stay nullable (a real, meaningful "no explicit choice yet" value that
    // Reset/equality-checks/the Changed payload all need to see); these two
    // additionally fall back to the SAME culture-derived resolution GanttChart
    // itself would apply (GanttScale.DefaultOffDays; DateTimeFormatInfo's own
    // FirstDayOfWeek), so the menu never visually LIES about which day/days
    // are actually in effect just because nobody has touched Region yet.
    private DayOfWeek DisplayFirstDayOfWeek => EffectiveFirstDayOfWeek ?? CultureInfo.CurrentCulture.DateTimeFormat.FirstDayOfWeek;
    private IReadOnlySet<DayOfWeek> DisplayOffDays => EffectiveOffDays ?? GanttScale.DefaultOffDays(CultureInfo.CurrentCulture);

    private static readonly DayOfWeek[] WeekDays =
    {
        DayOfWeek.Sunday, DayOfWeek.Monday, DayOfWeek.Tuesday, DayOfWeek.Wednesday,
        DayOfWeek.Thursday, DayOfWeek.Friday, DayOfWeek.Saturday,
    };

    private static string AbbreviatedDayName(DayOfWeek day) => CultureInfo.CurrentCulture.DateTimeFormat.AbbreviatedDayNames[(int)day];
    private static string FullDayName(DayOfWeek day) => CultureInfo.CurrentCulture.DateTimeFormat.DayNames[(int)day];

    // Forces the popover BODY's @key to change on every COMMITTED toggle
    // attempt (see the markup's own remarks) — a real Blazor rendering
    // quirk, found and fixed during this task's own disable-check pass, not
    // a hypothetical: Checkbox.razor's own Toggle() (a house component this
    // task does not own/modify — out of scope to change its fundamental
    // click mechanism) MUTATES ITS OWN `Checked` PARAMETER DIRECTLY as a C#
    // field assignment (`Checked = !Checked;`) BEFORE invoking
    // CheckedChanged — bypassing Blazor's own parameter-diffing bookkeeping
    // entirely. When a CONTROLLED consumer vetoes the click by re-supplying
    // the EXACT SAME value it handed down before the click (this component's
    // own controlled-veto contract, see the class remarks), the newly
    // computed EffectiveX value is IDENTICAL to what THIS component last
    // rendered — Blazor's diff (RenderTreeDiffBuilder comparing THIS
    // component's OWN successive render frames, not the child's current
    // in-memory field) sees "no change" at that frame position and skips
    // re-invoking SetParametersAsync on the child Checkbox entirely, leaving
    // its self-mutated (now-stale) `Checked` untouched — the checkbox stays
    // VISUALLY toggled even though the veto "worked" at the data layer (the
    // Changed callback still fired with the correct value; only the CHILD'S
    // OWN redundant, bypassed shadow copy is wrong). A changing @key forces
    // Blazor to tear down and recreate the keyed subtree as a logically NEW
    // instance on every commit (vetoed or not) instead of patching the old
    // one in place — a fresh instance always starts from whatever `Checked`
    // THIS render actually computes, with no stale shadow copy to carry
    // forward. Harmless in the non-veto case too (a genuine value change
    // recreates the control showing the correct, already-intended value).
    private int _commitGeneration;

    // Shared write path for every plain-bool setting (design spec Phase 3,
    // T8 — see the class remarks for the HasDelegate-gated controlled-ness
    // idiom this implements). Idempotent w.r.t. notification — a no-op when
    // `next` already matches the CURRENT effective value — mirroring
    // GanttState's own mutator discipline throughout this campaign; this is
    // what makes ResetAsync below safe to call unconditionally against every
    // setting without spamming Changed for the ones already at baseline.
    // Bumps _commitGeneration (see its own remarks) on every GENUINE commit
    // attempt — after the no-op guard, so a truly redundant call (next
    // already matches) doesn't remount the panel for nothing.
    private async Task SetBoolAsync(bool current, bool next, Action<bool> writeInternal, EventCallback<bool> changed)
    {
        if (current == next) return;
        _commitGeneration++;
        if (!changed.HasDelegate) writeInternal(next);
        StateHasChanged();
        if (changed.HasDelegate) await changed.InvokeAsync(next);
    }

    private Task SetShowRowCheckboxesAsync(bool v) => SetBoolAsync(EffectiveShowRowCheckboxes, v, x => _internalShowRowCheckboxes = x, ShowRowCheckboxesChanged);
    private Task SetShowSummaryBarsAsync(bool v) => SetBoolAsync(EffectiveShowSummaryBars, v, x => _internalShowSummaryBars = x, ShowSummaryBarsChanged);
    private Task SetShowZoomControlAsync(bool v) => SetBoolAsync(EffectiveShowZoomControl, v, x => _internalShowZoomControl = x, ShowZoomControlChanged);
    private Task SetShowOffscreenIndicatorsAsync(bool v) => SetBoolAsync(EffectiveShowOffscreenIndicators, v, x => _internalShowOffscreenIndicators = x, ShowOffscreenIndicatorsChanged);
    private Task SetInfiniteScrollAsync(bool v) => SetBoolAsync(EffectiveInfiniteScroll, v, x => _internalInfiniteScroll = x, InfiniteScrollChanged);
    private Task SetNowIndicatorAsync(bool v) => SetBoolAsync(EffectiveNowIndicator, v, x => _internalNowIndicator = x, NowIndicatorChanged);
    private Task SetMarkOffDaysAsync(bool v) => SetBoolAsync(EffectiveMarkOffDays, v, x => _internalMarkOffDays = x, MarkOffDaysChanged);
    private Task SetReadonlyAsync(bool v) => SetBoolAsync(EffectiveReadonly, v, x => _internalReadonly = x, ReadonlyChanged);
    private Task SetAllowCreateAsync(bool v) => SetBoolAsync(EffectiveAllowCreate, v, x => _internalAllowCreate = x, AllowCreateChanged);
    private Task SetAllowRowReorderAsync(bool v) => SetBoolAsync(EffectiveAllowRowReorder, v, x => _internalAllowRowReorder = x, AllowRowReorderChanged);
    private Task SetColorByGroupAsync(bool v) => SetBoolAsync(EffectiveColorByGroup, v, x => _internalColorByGroup = x, ColorByGroupChanged);

    private async Task SetFirstDayOfWeekAsync(DayOfWeek? next)
    {
        if (EffectiveFirstDayOfWeek == next) return;
        _commitGeneration++; // see its own remarks — forces a fresh ToggleGroup instance so a vetoed pick can't leave the just-clicked item visually stuck pressed
        if (FirstDayOfWeek is null) _internalFirstDayOfWeek = next; // nullability decides control (see class remarks and TreePaneWidth's own identical precedent) — a null FirstDayOfWeek is ALWAYS treated as uncontrolled, with NO way to distinguish "explicitly passed null" from "never set" (nullable value types carry no such signal) — so a genuine veto requires a NON-null controlled value (e.g. a consumer pinned at DayOfWeek.Monday ignoring further changes), exactly mirroring why TreePaneWidth's own veto only works for a real committed width, never for "null width"
        StateHasChanged();
        if (FirstDayOfWeekChanged.HasDelegate) await FirstDayOfWeekChanged.InvokeAsync(next);
    }

    private Task HandleFirstDayOfWeekToggledAsync(string? value) =>
        SetFirstDayOfWeekAsync(!string.IsNullOrEmpty(value) && Enum.TryParse<DayOfWeek>(value, out var parsed) ? parsed : null);

    private async Task SetOffDaysAsync(IReadOnlySet<DayOfWeek>? next)
    {
        if (SetEquals(EffectiveOffDays, next)) return;
        _commitGeneration++; // see its own remarks
        if (OffDays is null) _internalOffDays = next;
        StateHasChanged();
        if (OffDaysChanged.HasDelegate) await OffDaysChanged.InvokeAsync(next);
    }

    // Every day toggled OFF is a genuinely different value from "unset" —
    // "zero off days" is a real, deliberate choice (e.g. a 7-day operation)
    // a user can reach by clearing every chip, and it must NOT be silently
    // reinterpreted as null/culture-derived (which would reintroduce
    // Sat/Sun — or whatever the culture's own weekend is — right back after
    // the user explicitly cleared them). Represented as an explicit EMPTY
    // set, never null, from this handler.
    private Task HandleOffDaysToggledAsync(IEnumerable<string> values)
    {
        var days = new HashSet<DayOfWeek>();
        foreach (var v in values)
        {
            if (Enum.TryParse<DayOfWeek>(v, out var parsed)) days.Add(parsed);
        }
        return SetOffDaysAsync(days);
    }

    private static bool SetEquals(IReadOnlySet<DayOfWeek>? a, IReadOnlySet<DayOfWeek>? b)
    {
        if (a is null || b is null) return a is null && b is null;
        if (b is null) return false;
        return a.SetEquals(b);
    }

    // Design spec Phase 3, T8, decision 2 (see the class remarks for the full
    // "mount-time snapshot, not the library's hardcoded defaults" reasoning).
    // Captured exactly once, in OnInitialized — Blazor has already applied
    // every [Parameter] from SetParametersAsync by the time OnInitialized
    // runs, so EffectiveX here reads the value this instance actually opened
    // with (controlled: the consumer's own initial bound value; uncontrolled:
    // this component's own declared default — see the class remarks for why
    // those coincide in the uncontrolled case).
    private readonly record struct Baseline(
        bool ShowRowCheckboxes, bool ShowSummaryBars, bool ShowZoomControl, bool ShowOffscreenIndicators,
        bool InfiniteScroll, bool NowIndicator, bool MarkOffDays, bool Readonly, bool AllowCreate, bool AllowRowReorder, bool ColorByGroup,
        DayOfWeek? FirstDayOfWeek, IReadOnlySet<DayOfWeek>? OffDays);

    private Baseline _baseline;

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        _baseline = new Baseline(
            EffectiveShowRowCheckboxes, EffectiveShowSummaryBars, EffectiveShowZoomControl, EffectiveShowOffscreenIndicators,
            EffectiveInfiniteScroll, EffectiveNowIndicator, EffectiveMarkOffDays, EffectiveReadonly, EffectiveAllowCreate, EffectiveAllowRowReorder, EffectiveColorByGroup,
            EffectiveFirstDayOfWeek,
            // Bug fix (Codex review, P2 #10): every OTHER Baseline field above
            // is a value type (bool/DayOfWeek?), copied by value automatically
            // — but EffectiveOffDays can resolve to the CONTROLLED `OffDays`
            // parameter, a consumer-owned `IReadOnlySet<DayOfWeek>` reference.
            // Storing that reference directly (not a copy) meant a controlled
            // caller mutating its OWN set in place (e.g. `myOffDays.Add(...)`,
            // with no new Blazor render/parameter re-assignment) silently
            // mutated this "mount-time" baseline too — Reset would then
            // restore whatever the set currently holds, or (observed in
            // practice — see GanttV3Phase3T8Tests' own regression test) no-op
            // entirely: SetOffDaysAsync's own `SetEquals(EffectiveOffDays, next)`
            // short-circuit compares the (mutated) live set against the
            // (identically mutated, same-reference) "baseline" and finds them
            // equal, so it never even calls OffDaysChanged. Clone it into a
            // genuinely independent snapshot instead.
            EffectiveOffDays is null ? null : new HashSet<DayOfWeek>(EffectiveOffDays));
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// "Reset to defaults" (design spec Phase 3, T8) — restores every setting to
    /// this instance's own mount-time <see cref="Baseline"/> (see the class
    /// remarks, decision 2). Routes through the SAME setters every toggle click
    /// uses, so a controlled setting's own veto contract applies identically here
    /// (a consumer that ignores the resulting Changed callback keeps its current
    /// value on the next render, exactly like any other controlled/uncontrolled
    /// pair in this campaign) — this is a request, not a guaranteed mutation.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task ResetAsync()
    {
        await SetShowRowCheckboxesAsync(_baseline.ShowRowCheckboxes);
        await SetShowSummaryBarsAsync(_baseline.ShowSummaryBars);
        await SetShowZoomControlAsync(_baseline.ShowZoomControl);
        await SetShowOffscreenIndicatorsAsync(_baseline.ShowOffscreenIndicators);
        await SetInfiniteScrollAsync(_baseline.InfiniteScroll);
        await SetNowIndicatorAsync(_baseline.NowIndicator);
        await SetMarkOffDaysAsync(_baseline.MarkOffDays);
        await SetReadonlyAsync(_baseline.Readonly);
        await SetAllowCreateAsync(_baseline.AllowCreate);
        await SetAllowRowReorderAsync(_baseline.AllowRowReorder);
        await SetColorByGroupAsync(_baseline.ColorByGroup);
        await SetFirstDayOfWeekAsync(_baseline.FirstDayOfWeek);
        await SetOffDaysAsync(_baseline.OffDays);
    }
}
